src/backend/access/prolly/README

Prolly tree storage
===================

This directory holds prolly-tree storage for Postgres: table and index
access methods whose data lives in content-addressed trees, so that tables
can be branched, diffed, merged, cloned, pushed and pulled.

Status: every interface exists.  The foundation interfaces (node format,
chunker, content hash, canonical key and value encoders), the cluster-wide
node store, D1's WAL form, copy-on-write trees, the root layout, table
keying, row identity, the serial commit pipeline, the single_writer
coordination and engine D1 have real implementations that pass their
conformance suites; everything else is a stub.  prolly_d1 tables store
data: inserts, updates, deletes, row locks, ON CONFLICT, sequential, TID
and index scans (with btree and the other index AMs), and index builds
work; ANALYZE, sampling, bitmap scans and CLUSTER come next.


Interfaces and implementations
------------------------------

Every component is an interface with implementations behind it, so that any
design choice can be changed later by writing a new implementation:

	Interface         Header                  Implementations so far
	engine            prolly_engine.h         d1; d2 d3a d3b d4 (stubs)
	coordination      prolly_coord.h          single_writer; k1 k2 k3 (stubs)
	node_format       prolly_nodeformat.h     basic, stub
	chunker           prolly_chunker.h        keyhash, stub
	content_hash      prolly_hash.h           sha256, stub
	key_encoder       prolly_keyenc.h         ordered, stub
	value_encoder     prolly_valenc.h         canonical, heaptuple, stub
	node_store        prolly_nodestore.h      global, stub
	tree              prolly_tree.h           cow, stub
	root_layout       prolly_rootlayout.h     subroots, stub
	table_keying      prolly_keying.h         pk_or_content, stub
	allocator         prolly_alloc.h          stub
	reclamation       prolly_reclaim.h        stub
	log               prolly_log.h            chunks, stub
	commit_pipeline   prolly_commit.h         serial, stub
	row_identity      prolly_rowid.h          counter, stub
	refs              prolly_refs.h           stub
	transfer          prolly_transfer.h       stub
	diff_merge        prolly_merge.h          stub

An interface header declares a routine struct: a ProllyImplInfo (interface,
name, impl_id, format_version) followed only by callback pointers, with the
contract written as comments.  An implementation is one or more source files
here, named <interface>_<implementation>.c, that define a const routine
struct.  Nothing else includes an implementation's private headers or calls
its functions.

Callers go through PROLLY_CALL(routine, callback, args...) (prolly_impl.h).
A NULL callback means "not implemented", and PROLLY_CALL raises
ERRCODE_FEATURE_NOT_SUPPORTED naming the interface, implementation and
callback.  So a stub is a routine with every callback NULL, and a real
implementation can be filled in one callback at a time.

Built-in implementations are listed in src/include/access/prolly_impllist.h,
the one place that names them, and found by interface and name through the
registry (prolly_registry.c).  Libraries in shared_preload_libraries can
register more with prolly_register_impl().

Which implementation a tree uses comes from configuration (prolly_config.c),
never from code.  Today that is a default profile per table AM:

	Table AM      Engine   Coordination
	prolly_d1     d1       k1
	prolly_d2     d2       k1
	prolly_d3a    d3a      k2
	prolly_d3b    d3b      k2
	prolly_d4     d4       k3

All five share the same foundation: node format basic, chunker keyhash,
content hash sha256, key encoder ordered, value encoder canonical, table
keying pk_or_content, and stubs for the interfaces without an
implementation yet.  prolly_d1 and prolly_d2 use the node store global,
the tree cow, the root layout subroots, the commit pipeline serial and
the row identity counter; prolly_d1 also the log chunks, and the
coordination single_writer instead of k1 until k1 is built.


Canonical values
----------------

Rows and keys are stored in canonical form: the same bytes on every
platform, in every cluster and session, whatever the table's DDL history,
with no local OIDs.  prolly_datum.c holds
the canonical encoding of single values, shared by the key and value
encoders as a fixed part of the format: send formats produced with the
client encoding fixed to UTF-8 (SetClientEncodingOverride in mbutils.c),
and OID-free encodings of arrays and composites.  valenc_canonical.c stores
live columns only, without trailing NULLs.  DROP COLUMN doesn't rewrite a
table, so rows from earlier layouts remain: encoders describe their row
layout, and decode rows encoded under an earlier one.  heaptuple stays registered for
comparison and fails the platform and history checks by design.


Storage and WAL
---------------

All nodes, of every relation in every database on every branch, live in one
cluster-wide store (nodestore_global.c): relation files 9393 (nodes), 9394
(hash -> block map) and 9395 (map overflow) in the global tablespace,
through smgr and the buffer manager.  Internal nodes carry an unhashed
block hint per child, so reads usually skip the map.

Two WAL resource managers carry prolly records (prolly_xlog.h,
prolly_rmgr.c): Prolly for the log interface (D1's "chunks" logs each new
node as a full page image) and ProllyStore for the node store's own map.
Each record names the implementation that wrote it, and redo dispatches to
it.  test_prolly's TAP test replays both on a streaming standby and through
crash recovery under wal_consistency_checking.

Database-wide components (refs, transfer, diff_merge) come from a database
profile.  Once a tree can be created, its components will be recorded in the
tree with their format stamps and read back from there, so changing
configuration never changes how existing data is read.


Trees
-----

A tree is an immutable sorted map identified by its root node, made of the
foundation components an engine passes in (prolly_tree.h).  tree_cow.c
builds trees bottom up, cutting each level into nodes with the chunker; the
root is the first level with one node, so the shape and every hash depend
on the items alone.  apply() takes a sorted batch of edits and re-chunks a
window around each one, a level at a time, until the new boundaries meet an
old one; everything after that point is shared with the old tree.  Internal
items carry the child's hash, its item count and (as the node format's
unhashed hint) its block, and every node caches its own hash in its special
space, so a stale hint falls back to the hash -> block map.


Engine D1
---------

A database's working set is one branch root (root layout subroots): a
catalog, a user-data and a local sub-root, each mapping relfilenumbers to
trees.  A relation's data tree is in the catalog or user-data sub-root;
its local metadata (row identity map, recorded keying) shares one tree in
the local sub-root, each key prefixed by its kind.  Keying by
relfilenumber makes each new storage (TRUNCATE, rewrites, REINDEX) a new
tree, so rollback and file swaps behave as on heap.

A transaction's writes stay in memory, in a write set per tree: a
red-black tree of keys, each with its versions tagged by command id and
subtransaction.  Cursors merge the base tree with the write set, seeing a
key's newest version from an earlier command.  Aborting a subtransaction
drops its versions, write sets it created, and undoes emptyings it did.
At commit, xact_precommit takes the branch lock, applies every write
set's newest versions to the tip's trees, builds the new root and
enqueues it; xact_commit publishes it.  Writers hold the right to write
a table from coordination single_writer (below) from before their first
write until they end, so a write set's base, read from the newest head
when it's made, doesn't change under it.  Readers see the root set they
hold with the transaction's own writes over it; snapshots read the newest
published head.  Rows rebuilt under another encoding are written with
InvalidCommandId and seen by every command.


Coordination "single_writer"
----------------------------

One writer per table: a heavyweight lock of its own (LOCKTAG_PROLLY_TABLE,
pg_locks type prollytable), separate from the relation's locks, taken in
ExclusiveLock by writes, FOR UPDATE and FOR NO KEY UPDATE, and ShareLock by
FOR SHARE and FOR KEY SHARE, held to the end of the (sub)transaction.
Waiting for it means another writer may have committed, so the table AM
reads again after it gets it.


Commit pipeline "serial"
------------------------

Branch heads live in shared memory under ProllyHeadsLock.  The branch
lock is a heavyweight lock (LOCKTAG_PROLLY_BRANCH) held until the
committer's transaction ends.  enqueue() adds a pending entry and writes a
ProllyCommit record with the root and xid, before the commit record;
publish() makes it the head.  After a crash or on a standby, pending
entries are settled through the commit log.  Checkpoints write heads and
pending entries to global/prolly_heads, read back at startup.

Heads are kept per database.  CREATE DATABASE copies the template's
heads to the new database as pending entries of the creating
transaction, published when it commits and dropped when it aborts, and
DROP DATABASE forgets the database's heads (both WAL-logged), through
prolly_database.c.  Entries nobody settles (a crash's, or a standby's
that nobody read before promotion) are settled by the next checkpoint on
a primary; never during recovery, where a transaction whose commit is
still to be replayed can look finished.

The heads table holds at most 1024 (database, branch) pairs across the
cluster.  A commit that needs a new head reserves its slot when it
enqueues its root, so a full table fails the transaction before it
commits instead of after (which would also fail every replay of it).
debug_prolly_max_branch_heads lowers the limit for tests.  Redo settles
a branch's earlier entry before adding its new one, so it holds one entry
per branch, and when the pending table is full it settles the entries
whose commit or abort it has replayed.  Keeping heads
in the store, with shared memory as a cache, comes with branches.  Every prolly relation also has
an empty main fork, so the copy strategies, ALTER TABLE SET TABLESPACE
and the size functions find the file they expect.


Rows, keys and TIDs
-------------------

Table keying (keying_pkcontent.c) keys a table by its primary key, or
by row content hash with a count of identical rows.  Each data tree
records the keying description it was written with (META kind); scans
read with it, and the first write in a (sub)transaction after the keying
changed (primary key added or dropped, or a rewrite through a table
without one) rebuilds the tree.  The description records the key
columns' types, so rows keyed by a column dropped since (or by a type
dropped with it) are still read; the key encoder starts only when keys
are compared or encoded.  When a key's type, operator family or
collation has been dropped, no keying can order its keys any more
(can_compare is false); D1 then matches the write set's keys with the
tree's by their bytes, which serves full scans and the rebuild, and
stores a commit made without writing again as a tree in byte order.

Row identity (rowid_counter.c) gives each row version (key, ordinal among
identical rows) a TID from a per-table counter in block 0 of the table's
main fork, reserved 1024 at a time with generic WAL, so a TID is never
given out twice, even after the transaction that got it aborts (indexes
keep entries for it) and on any branch.  The map holds TID -> version
(content hash, ordinal, xmin, key) and (key, ordinal) -> TID.  An update
or delete retires the old TID, recording its successor (for READ
COMMITTED's update chains and currtid) and its row, which SnapshotAny
returns as heap returns a dead tuple.  Rebuilding a tree renames TIDs
rather than giving new ones, so indexes stay valid.

The table AM's rows by TID: an update, delete or row lock first gets the
right to write or lock the row, then compares the row as the command sees
it with every write and with what others committed: live, changed by this
transaction (TM_SelfModified), or changed by a transaction that committed
while it waited (TM_Updated with the successor, TM_Deleted).  Slots are
virtual slots that look up xmin, and whether the row is the current
transaction's, through the map.  Uniqueness checks find TIDs in indexes
that no map knows (another writer's private rows) and wait for the right
to write the table before looking again.


Core hooks
----------

prolly_shmem.c is a shared-memory subsystem (subsystemlist.h) reserving
an area per engine, coordination and commit pipeline implementation that
asks for one, and CheckPointProlly() runs in CheckPointGuts().
createdb() and dropdb() call ProllyCopyDatabase() and
ProllyDropDatabase() (prolly_database.c).  A table
AM may leave the parallel-scan callbacks NULL; the planner then keeps
scans of its tables in the leader (AMFLAG_NO_PARALLEL_SCAN), as for
temporary tables, because workers couldn't see the leader's private
writes.


Adapters
--------

The Postgres-facing code is thin and holds no engine logic:

	prolly_tableam.c   one TableAmRoutine for all prolly table AMs
	prolly_indexam.c   the "prolly" index AM; its opclasses follow the btree
	                   contract, so validation is btvalidate()
	prolly_vcs.c       SQL functions prolly_branch_create, prolly_checkout,
	                   prolly_commit, prolly_diff, prolly_merge, prolly_clone,
	                   prolly_fetch, prolly_push

Table AM callbacks not built yet raise "does not implement ... yet".
prolly_xact.c delivers transaction events to engines with private
writes.

The catalog entries (pg_am, pg_proc, pg_opfamily, pg_opclass, pg_amop,
pg_amproc) use OIDs 9370-9392.


Tests
-----

src/test/modules/test_prolly runs each interface's conformance suite
against every registered implementation, and reaches each stub from SQL:

	make -C src/test/modules/test_prolly check

which also runs d1_tables (D1 tables from SQL) and the TAP tests: node
store replay (001) and branch heads across crashes, checkpoints, standby
replay and promotion (002).
